WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.237
Here is the learning path for this module.

00:00:02.237 --> 00:00:04.682
We begin with the core aggregate functions: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, and MAX.

00:00:04.682 --> 00:00:11.472
Then we use GROUP BY to create one summary row for each category or combination of categories.

00:00:11.472 --> 00:00:13.952
After that, we separate WHERE from HAVING.

00:00:13.952 --> 00:00:16.562
WHERE decides which source rows are allowed into the aggregation.

00:00:16.562 --> 00:00:19.097
HAVING decides which completed groups stay in the final result.

00:00:19.097 --> 00:00:24.087
We then extend the same ideas with conditional aggregation, PostgreSQL FILTER, ROLLUP, CUBE, GROUPING SETS,

00:00:24.087 --> 00:00:27.037
and window functions. The practice pattern also becomes gradually more independent.

00:00:27.037 --> 00:00:30.792
First you run complete examples, then change them, then fill in missing pieces,

00:00:30.792 --> 00:00:34.097
and finally write short analytical queries yourself.

00:00:34.097 --> 00:00:37.672
Connect this idea to a student-success report comparing course-level outcomes.

00:00:37.672 --> 00:00:42.877
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:42.877 --> 00:00:48.737
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:48.737 --> 00:00:51.442
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.442 --> 00:00:56.000
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
